' Notes on the generated domain types. Each claim is from the type's own
' header.
note right of "ores::iam::domain::account"
Bi-temporal and audited (domain/account.hpp): version, the four audit
columns and the valid_from/valid_to pair with the GIST exclusion. One row
per user, service, algorithm or LLM identity; username is the natural key,
unique on (tenant_id, username), and account_type defaults to "user".
image_id, reports_to_account_id and default_party_id are nullable uuid soft
references held as optional, and reports_to_account_id makes the type
self-referencing. The table is a composite parent:
ores_iam_accounts_touch_version_fn lets account contact information or an
account-party row bump this account's version when the child is written.
service_password_hash is a real column with no domain member, reached only
by check_service_credentials, so it never travels on the wire. This entity
is read_only: the generated account_protocol.hpp owns the reads
(iam.v1.accounts.list, .get and the version reads) and the hand-written
account_operations_protocol.hpp owns the writes.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::session"
TimescaleDB hypertable partitioned by start_time with 7-day chunks
(domain/session.hpp). It has no version column, no audit tail and no
valid_from/valid_to, and it keys on (id, start_time) rather than a single
surrogate key, because a hypertable's partition column must sit in its
primary key. end_time is text not null default '', not a nullable
timestamp, so an active session carries the empty string; client_ip is
text, not inet. The hand-written struct's party_id, visible_party_ids and
username have no backing column and now live on session_view.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::tenant"
Core entity for multi-tenancy (domain/tenant.hpp). The system tenant's id
is the maximum UUID value, ffffffff-ffff-ffff-ffff-ffffffffffff, and it
owns shared reference data; SQL also carries tenant_id = id as a
self-reference. A tenant is addressed by id, by its unique code ('system',
'acme') or by its unique hostname, which routes a login to a tenant. type
is a foreign key to tenant_types and status a foreign key to
tenant_statuses; both are strings here.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::login_info"
Current-state table, one row per account keyed by account_id
(domain/login_info.hpp): no valid_from/valid_to, no version column and no
audit tail, unlike the bi-temporal types beside it. last_login, last_ip,
last_attempt_ip, failed_logins, locked, online and password_reset_required
are the login history and security state.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::permission"
Temporal but unaudited (domain/permission.hpp): it keeps the
valid_from/valid_to window, the GIST exclusion and the delete rule, and has
no version column and no audit tail, because permissions are
system-defined constants seeded from bootstrap data rather than user-edited
records. code is the atomic grant, named component::resource:action.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::role"
Bi-temporal and audited (domain/role.hpp). name is the natural key, unique
on (tenant_id, name). The hand-written struct also carried permission_codes,
which no column backs: it is denormalised from ores_iam_role_permissions_tbl
by an RBAC join, so it was removed from the entity and the join stays in
the hand-written authorization layer. The generated role_protocol.hpp still
declares the entity CRUD messages; only the competing handler is switched
off, because the hand-written role_handler owns iam.v1.roles.*.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::account_contact_information"
Real name and contact details for an account
(domain/account_contact_information.hpp). One record per account rather
than one per contact type: a person needs no
Legal/Operations/Settlement/Billing split. The account's real name lives
on account.full_name, not here; this entity answers only how to reach the
holder. It is a child of account, so writing it bumps the account's own
version through the touch function.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::account_party"
Junction table linking an IAM account to a party (domain/account_party.hpp).
One account may hold several parties, which controls the parties a user can
act on behalf of. party_id is a plain uuid: the party itself is
ores.refdata's entity, and no party type is declared in this component.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::account_role"
Junction entity linking accounts to roles (domain/account_role.hpp), the
many-to-many grant: one account holds many roles and one role is held by
many accounts. assigned_by records who granted it, and it carries no
version column.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::domain::role_permission"
Junction entity linking roles to permissions (domain/role_permission.hpp).
It carries tenant_id, role_id and permission_id and nothing else; the RBAC
join that reads it is what produces an account's effective permission
codes.
end note

