' Notes on the messaging facet.
note top of "ores::iam::messaging::list_account_contact_informations_request"
Every request struct declares a using response_type alias naming its paired
response, and a static constexpr string_view nats_subject. The automated
pass skips using and static lines, so neither arrives. A CRUD subject reads
iam.v1.RESOURCE.VERB with VERB one of list, get, get_many, put, put_many,
delete or delete_many, and the matching version subject reads
iam.v1.RESOURCE_versions.VERB. The remaining subjects carry a descriptive or
hyphenated verb instead: auth.login, auth.logout, auth.refresh,
auth.public-key, auth.service-login, auth.signup, accounts.save,
accounts.update, accounts.delete, accounts.lock, accounts.unlock,
accounts.change-password, accounts.reset-password, accounts.update-email,
accounts.select-party, accounts.switch-party, accounts.set-default-party,
accounts.publish-from-dq, account_parties.list_by_account_id,
roles.assign, roles.assign-by-name, roles.revoke, roles.revoke-by-name,
roles.by-account, roles.permissions, roles.permissions-by-account,
roles.suggest-commands, sessions.active, sessions.samples,
bootstrap.status, bootstrap.create-admin, bootstrap.provision-tenant,
system.reset, system.reset-tenant, tenants.complete-provisioning and
tenants.provision-acme. Most requests also declare a static
requires_session, true wherever the caller must already hold a session and
false for the seven operations that produce one: login, signup,
service-login, public-key, bootstrap status, bootstrap create-admin and
bootstrap provision-tenant.
end note
note bottom of "ores::iam::messaging::publish_account_contact_informations_from_dq_request"
The automated pass reads this subject wrong: the static declaration wraps
onto a second line and the pass takes only the first, so the member never
arrives. It is the only wrapped member declaration in the component. Both
publish-from-dq structs are triggers rather than requests: each states a
subject and no response_type, and each body is empty because the
server-side function knows how to expand the DQ artefact. This one's
subject, iam.v1.account-contact-informations.publish-from-dq, hyphenates the
resource where every other subject for the same entity uses underscores
(iam.v1.account_contact_informations.list and its siblings).
end note
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::switch_party_request"
Deliberately a separate subject from select_party
(account_operations_protocol.hpp): select_party accepts only the
narrowly-scoped, single-use token with audience "select_party_only" that
the login flow issues, while switch_party accepts a normal
already-authenticated session token, so an account with access to several
parties can change the party in view mid-session without logging out.
Membership check and token issuance are otherwise the same, which is why
switch_party_request names select_party_response as its response_type.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::login_response"
login is the one protocol whose request does not require a session, since
it is what produces one (login_protocol.hpp). session_id is the IAM session
uuid created for this login and matches a row in ores_iam_sessions_tbl; the
header tells clients to forward it as Nats-Session-Id on every later
request so one login's calls correlate in the logs. access_lifetime_s
defaults to 1800 and lets a client arm its proactive refresh timer from the
server's own configuration, and available_parties carries one
party_summary per party the account may act on.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::session_view"
The party-scoped shape of a session (session_operations_protocol.hpp).
session is the entity; party_id, visible_party_ids and username are
denormalised fields reached through the account-party association, and no
column backs any of them, so they are message fields rather than domain
members. get_active_sessions_response returns domain sessions, not views.
end note
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::account_event_subjects"
Every entity family declares a namespace of three subject constants, one
per action, because an event reports what happened and no caller asked for
it: the payload is the family's own event struct and the subject's last
segment is the action. The other nine are
account_contact_information_, account_type_, login_info_, permission_,
role_, session_, tenant_, tenant_status_ and tenant_type_event_subjects.
Each underscore suffix matches the subject_prefix its entity_event_traits
states.
end note

